 subsets so we had one set of  urgent care  samples so these were plasma samples  taken from people that  visited for example emergency  departments and that sought acute care  and these were likely arranged for  um acute source could be two in fact in  particular during  um the early pandemic peak which was uh  during which time  the the regular care was also used quite  considerably  we also had a second group of patients  so these were in the routine care  group these were people coming in for  follow-up appointments for for example  prenatal testing  and these more closely represented the  general population  and these two sample cells were roughly  50 50  distributed so i'll show you some of the  results of the cross-sectional analysis  first  um so the two plots that i'm showing  here on the left  are the results for the urgent care  group and these were essentially  our positive controls for this analysis  and then on the right  are the same results now for the routine  care group so what we see for the urgent  care group is that  consistent with the early pandemic peaks  or the early surge of the virus  in late march and then beginning of  april we see that  very closely reflected within the zero  positivity  of our panel with an offset of about two  weeks which is the time  that it takes for um for antibody  responses to become  uh undetectable um  what we then see is that during the  summer months when we saw very few acute  cases  we see this frequency reduced to about  20 to 25  at the same time we also see a similar  increase in the routine care group  this is a more gradual increase until  about mid april  at which point um the the frequency  levels off to about 20  which is very similar to what we saw  during the summer months for the urgent  care group as well  so what these data tell us are two  things first  is that during the first spanish peak  about one in five new yorkers were  exposed  to the virus what it also tells us if we  look at the early period  between february and mid-march is that  there was  between two percent um  up to two percent of cases uh had  already evidence of prior infections  with govid19  so we don't know whether these  individuals were  exposed while they were in new york or  where they were exposed  prior to for example travel to new york  but it does tell us that there was  already detectable  serial converted individuals within the  city during the month of  february so of course we wanted to back  this up with molecular evidence  uh to see whether we could detect the  presence of virus  uh within uh the same patient  populations and for this we use the  second set of samples so these were the  3000 samples  of individuals that tested negative for  no respiratory various pathogens  and that we then pulled in sets of 10  and  re-screened on the roche cobalt system  to do an rtq pcr for code to  vital rna so this particular test has  two different probes  t1 and t2 each of which target a  different region of the surgical v2  genome  and we essentially identified positive  samples that had  detection of both of those targets as  well as presumptive positive  pools that have detection of at least  one of those targets  and we followed up both types of samples  so the results of that are shown here so  um  between january and march we tested  consecutive weeks  and the numbers here indicate the total  numbers of pools tested  and at the top of these bars we can see  how many presumptive positive  and how many positive samples were  detected  um we can look at that in a little bit  more detail so we're just highlighting  the percent of positive and opposite  specimens  you can see that the earliest detections  were as early as the  week ending in january 18th and january  25th  but the bulk of detections really come  at the end of february  and at the beginning of march we then  did additional sequencing of those  samples because of course we wanted a to  validate  um that these symbols indeed had viral  rna present parallel genomic rna present  but we also wanted to see if we could  reconstruct the genomes  and add them to the phylogenies of these  initial cases that we had already  profiled  um and the results of the sequencing are  shown here  so we had essentially uh several of the  initial samples confirmed  from the week ending in january 25th as  well as the week uh ending in february  first  so all those samples we were not able to  obtain complete genomes but we did  detect the presence of viral rna which  validates that we indeed have protection  b2 vital rna during this period of time  and then in the later sample sets from  the weeks ending on february 29th  as well as the weekend on march 7th we  were able to obtain complete genomes so  we then took those genomes  and placed them within the larger  phylogenetic tree of storage flow v2  and what that showed us is that in  addition to these early detections that  we saw on the weekend  january 25th and the weekend on the  february 1st  that we were not able to place in the  tree because we didn't obtain a complete  genome  we do see the in red here different  bulbs in red  the complete genomes that we obtained  prior to the surge of cases that are  highlighted here in yellow  that occurred mostly in march the  mid-march to end of march  of uh 2020. and what that tells us is  that  we have the earliest detection in the  week of january 25th but also that we  have multiple detections  between our sort of initial positive  cases that were identified based on  non-travel history  and the surge of cases that we saw in  mid-march  once the detection capability  so our ability to detect source code v2  at  higher numbers uh actually came online  so we had a brief window of time of  maybe about two weeks that if we had  uh widespread testing online earlier we  could have  detected communities but  prior to the large surge that we saw in  the weeks  proceeding  so of course we have not sit st